#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""mesh-job-act — the CONSUMER of `job-mail.log`'s `act` rows: a standing, aged liability ledger.

  mesh-job-act [--tg] [--json] [--quiet]
  mesh-job-act --discharge <uid|key> --verb <verb> [--why "..."] [--who <w>]
  mesh-job-act --test

# reflex-cadence: 17 * * * *
# reflex-args: --tg

WHY THIS EXISTS. `mesh-job-mail` classifies every incoming message into act / info / noise and
writes it to `~/.mesh/job-mail.log`. On 2026-08-29 a batch of employer contacts was found sitting
unanswered, the oldest well over a week old. The story told first was that a lane had gone dark —
the gmail/adb leg died 2026-08-21 with the Note 3's cable — and that the intake had been blind for
eight days. **That story is refuted by the tape.** The yandex lane ran every single day of that
stretch without a miss, and every one of those messages was read, classified `act` and ledgered on
the day it arrived. Nothing was blind.

What was missing is the other half: **nothing on this node had ever READ an `act` row.** Grepped
across the whole genome the string `job-mail` appeared in exactly one file — the tool that writes
it. The classifier was a detector whose only actuator was a one-shot TG push at the instant the
mail arrived; a push scrolls away, and after it the row had no standing anywhere. That is
CLAUDE.md's «a detector is not a closed loop» in its purest form: the loop's last hand was the
operator's, and a human's attention is exactly the thing that does not survive nine days.

So this tool is the consumer. It does NOT decide whether a message deserves an answer — that
judgement belongs to a mind or to the operator, and a classifier that silently decided «this one
needs nothing» would recreate the same blindness pointing the other way. It makes every `act` row
a LEVEL-triggered liability that ages in the open until something EXPLICITLY discharges it.

(Counts below describe the SHAPE of the corpus this was priced against — a join's reach, a family's
share. The operator's own funnel numbers and the employers he is talking to stay out of this
repository, which is public: `job/README.md`, «код лана — публичный, цифры поиска работы живого
человека — нет».)

FOUR THINGS THAT ARE NOT OBVIOUS, ALL MEASURED AGAINST THE LIVE CORPUS FIRST.

* **A name-keyed discharge matcher covers 1 row in 10, and assuming otherwise would have been the
  whole bug again.** The only machine tape that records an answer is `~/.mesh/job/reply-state.json`,
  keyed by vacancy id and carrying an employer name. Of the 9 non-`code` act rows in the corpus at
  build time only 3 carry a company at all (the other 6 read `-`, because hh's «Новое сообщение от
  работодателя» notification names no vacancy), and only ONE of those 3 had a reply-state entry.
  So the machine leg reached 1 row in 9, and **its coverage is published in every run** rather
  than assumed: a matcher correct for one family is a blindness in its sibling, and the honest
  default for the other nine is OPEN, never «probably handled».

* **`code` is a different family and must not share a bound.** Half the act rows are
  verification codes, whose life is minutes: they are consumed by a human typing them into a form
  and there is no tape of that anywhere. Ageing them as liabilities would have made this tool a
  permanent multi-line alarm from its first run — noise that trains its reader to skip it. A code
  therefore EXPIRES on its own window and is counted in its own column, never silenced.

* **The kind table is not an allowlist.** An `act` kind this tool has never heard of gets the
  TIGHTEST bound and is NAMED as unrecognised, because an exclusion list's failure direction is
  silence and the whole incident here was a silence. A future classifier kind alarms loudly on
  arrival instead of quietly never counting.

* **Age is CALENDAR and up-hours BOTH, and neither substitutes.** The employer really has waited
  nine days whether or not this node was powered — the liability is his wait, not our sampling. But
  the mesh could only ever have acted while UP, and this node power-cycles daily (6h25m off on
  2026-08-25 alone), so blame measured in calendar hours over-states what any reflex could have
  done. Both numbers are printed on every open row; collapsing them into one would hide either the
  operator's exposure or the node's real opportunity.

DISCHARGE. Three lanes, all explicit, none inferred from silence:
  1. `mesh-job-act --discharge <uid> --verb answered|no-reply-needed|operator-handling|wont-answer`
     writes an attributed row to `~/.mesh/job/act-discharge.tsv`. This is the lane that covers
     every row no machine tape can reach — which, measured, is most of them.
  2. `reply-state.json`, by employer name, with `sent` at or after the row — and `confirmed:false`
     does NOT clear it. A reply whose thread never confirmed renders `sent-unconfirmed` and stays
     open, because «клик прошёл» and «тред подтвердил» are two different worlds (da49ab85).
  3. the `code` family's own expiry window.

A row is written to `~/.mesh/job-act.log` on EVERY pass whether or not anything was open — a tape
of only positives is a numerator, and the silences here are the denominator that says this reflex
ran at all. An unreadable `job-mail.log` renders UNKNOWN and rc 2, never «0 open»: the one reading
this tool exists to make impossible is a fault wearing the words of an all-clear.

Loudness is the board and the operator's TG, throttled per key (`~/.mesh/job/act-alarm.json`), not
the exit code — red on a tract that is working teaches its reader to ignore red. A `[task]` per
open row was considered and REJECTED: it would mint one board promise per employer message, which
`mesh-promises` then carries as a leaking liability, and the hand that actually answers (the job
window, the operator) is already reading the room.
"""
import argparse, calendar, json, os, pathlib, re, signal, subprocess, sys, time

# SIGTERM MUST UNWIND — see mesh-job-mail's note. `timeout` signals the process GROUP, so every
# timeout-wrapped call site (mesh-doctor's --test sweep, mesh-autowire) delivers TERM here.
for _sig in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP):
    signal.signal(_sig, lambda _s, _f: sys.exit(128 + _s))

os.environ["PATH"] = os.path.expanduser("~/.local/bin") + os.pathsep + os.environ.get("PATH", "/usr/bin:/bin")

HOME = pathlib.Path.home()
LEDGER = HOME / ".mesh" / "job-mail.log"
REPLYSTATE = HOME / ".mesh" / "job" / "reply-state.json"
DISCHARGE = HOME / ".mesh" / "job" / "act-discharge.tsv"
RUNTAPE = HOME / ".mesh" / "job-act.log"
ALARM = HOME / ".mesh" / "job" / "act-alarm.json"
TG, CHAT = "mesh-tg", "mesh-chat"

# Bounds in CALENDAR hours, by family. These are DECLARED, not measured, and they say so in every
# reading (`src=declared`): the corpus of discharges was EMPTY at build time — nothing had ever been
# discharged, because nothing had ever consumed a row — so there is no distribution to calibrate
# against. The one answer latency on record at build time was n=1, about 20 minutes.
# When the discharge tape has grown its own history this becomes a measured p90; until then a
# constant that names itself a constant beats a constant dressed as a measurement.
BOUNDS = {"employer": 4.0, "employer?": 4.0, "invite": 4.0, "ats": 24.0}
UNKNOWN_KIND_BOUND = 4.0          # the tightest bound, on purpose: unlisted must fail LOUD
CODE_EXPIRY_MIN = float(os.environ.get("MESH_JOB_ACT_CODE_MIN", "30"))
THROTTLE_H = float(os.environ.get("MESH_JOB_ACT_THROTTLE_H", "6"))
VERBS = ("answered", "no-reply-needed", "operator-handling", "wont-answer")


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- time

def iso(t):
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(t))


def parse_iso(s):
    """Lenient on purpose: job-mail.log writes seconds, reply-state.json writes «...T15:33Z»."""
    s = (s or "").strip()
    for fmt in ("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%MZ"):
        try:
            return calendar.timegm(time.strptime(s, fmt))
        except Exception:
            pass
    return None


def up_windows():
    """[(start, end)] of every window this NODE was powered up, or None when the boot record is
    unreadable — a caller must then render up-hours `na`, never sum a missing record to zero
    downtime. `mesh-cron-catchup --boot-windows` is the mesh's ONE parser of `journalctl
    --list-boots`; a private second copy here would rot on its own. The CURRENT boot comes from
    /proc/uptime, because the journal's last_entry lags the clock by however long nothing logged
    and that lag would be counted as the machine being OFF."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["mesh-cron-catchup", "--boot-windows"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        if p.returncode != 0:
            return None
        w = [(float(a), float(b)) for a, b in json.loads(p.stdout)]
    except Exception:
        return None
    if not w:
        return None
    try:
        boot0 = time.time() - float(open("/proc/uptime").read().split()[0])
    except Exception:
        return None
    w = [(a, b) for a, b in w if b < boot0]
    w.append((boot0, time.time()))
    return sorted(w)


def up_hours(t0, t1, windows):
    """-> (hours POWERED UP in [t0,t1], floor?). floor=True when the boot record does not reach
    back to t0, so the sum is a LOWER bound — the safe direction for anything judged against it."""
    if windows is None or t0 is None or t1 is None:
        return None, False
    secs = sum(max(0.0, min(b, t1) - max(a, t0)) for a, b in windows)
    return secs / 3600.0, t0 < min(a for a, _ in windows)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the corpus

def act_rows(path=None):
    """Every `act` row of job-mail.log, deduped by uid on the EARLIEST sighting.

    -> (rows, error). error is not None when the ledger could not be read at all; the caller
    must then render UNKNOWN. A re-read of the same mailbox re-writes rows verbatim (uid 17802
    appears at both 15:13:28Z and 15:17:12Z in the live corpus), so the first sighting is the
    row's birthday and the pair (uid, first-seen) is its identity — uids restart when the
    mailbox's UIDVALIDITY rolls, and a bare uid key would let a discharge written today clear
    an unrelated message next month."""
    try:
        lines = pathlib.Path(path or LEDGER).read_text(errors="replace").splitlines()
    except Exception as e:
        return None, str(e)
    out = {}
    for ln in lines:
        f = ln.split("\t")
        if len(f) < 4 or f[2] != "act":
            continue
        t = parse_iso(f[0])
        if t is None:
            continue
        uid = f[1]
        row = {"key": None, "uid": uid, "seen": t, "kind": f[3],
               "company": f[4] if len(f) > 4 else "-", "role": f[5] if len(f) > 5 else "-",
               "subject": f[6] if len(f) > 6 else "", "gist": f[7] if len(f) > 7 else "",
               "lane": "gmail" if uid.startswith("gm:") else "yandex"}
        if uid not in out or t < out[uid]["seen"]:
            out[uid] = row
    for r in out.values():
        r["key"] = "%s@%s" % (r["uid"], iso(r["seen"]))
    return sorted(out.values(), key=lambda r: r["seen"]), None


def discharges(path=None):
    """The explicit discharge tape: ts \t key \t who \t verb \t why. Keyed on the full key, but
    a bare uid is honoured too so a human can type `--discharge 17886` — resolution happens at
    WRITE time (below), where an ambiguous uid is refused rather than guessed at."""
    out = {}
    try:
        lines = pathlib.Path(path or DISCHARGE).read_text().splitlines()
    except Exception:
        return out
    for ln in lines:
        f = ln.split("\t")
        if len(f) < 4:
            continue
        out[f[1]] = {"at": parse_iso(f[0]), "who": f[2], "verb": f[3],
                     "why": f[4] if len(f) > 4 else ""}
    return out


def reply_index(path=None):
    """employer-name -> the newest {sent, confirmed, title} in reply-state.json.

    This is the ONLY machine tape in the lane that records an answer, and it is keyed by vacancy
    id with the employer as a free-text field, so joining it to a mail row can only ever go
    through the name. Its coverage over the corpus is published, never assumed."""
    try:
        st = json.loads(pathlib.Path(path or REPLYSTATE).read_text())
    except Exception:
        return {}
    idx = {}
    for vid, e in (st or {}).items():
        name = norm(e.get("employer", ""))
        sent = parse_iso(e.get("sent", ""))
        if not name or sent is None:
            continue
        cur = idx.get(name)
        if cur is None or sent > cur["sent"]:
            idx[name] = {"sent": sent, "confirmed": bool(e.get("confirmed")),
                         "title": e.get("title", ""), "vid": vid}
    return idx


def norm(s):
    return re.sub(r"\s+", " ", (s or "").strip().lower())


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the verdict

def assess(row, dis, ridx, now, windows):
    """-> the row with its state, bound and both ages attached.

    states: discharged · sent-unconfirmed · expired · open · over
    `sent-unconfirmed` is deliberately NOT a discharge: mesh-job-reply can post a message and
    still fail to see it in the thread, and treating a click as an answer is the exact failure
    da49ab85 named THREE worlds to stop."""
    r = dict(row)
    age_h = (now - r["seen"]) / 3600.0
    uph, floor = up_hours(r["seen"], now, windows)
    r["age_h"], r["up_h"], r["up_floor"] = age_h, uph, floor

    if r["kind"] == "code":
        r["bound_h"], r["bound_src"] = CODE_EXPIRY_MIN / 60.0, "expiry(declared)"
        r["state"] = "expired" if age_h * 60.0 > CODE_EXPIRY_MIN else "open"
        r["why"] = "verification code — consumed by a human typing it, no tape of that exists"
        return r

    known = r["kind"] in BOUNDS
    r["bound_h"] = BOUNDS.get(r["kind"], UNKNOWN_KIND_BOUND)
    r["bound_src"] = "declared" if known else "declared(UNRECOGNISED KIND — tightest bound)"

    # ONLY the full key. A bare uid is honoured at WRITE time (do_discharge resolves it, refusing
    # an ambiguous one) and never at read time: a hand-edited row carrying a bare uid would clear
    # every row that uid ever names, which is the collision `uid@first-seen` exists to prevent —
    # uids restart when the mailbox's UIDVALIDITY rolls. An unrecognised discharge row therefore
    # leaves its target OPEN and LOUD, which is the safe direction for a silencer to fail in.
    d = dis.get(r["key"])
    if d:
        r["state"], r["why"] = "discharged", "%s by %s%s" % (
            d["verb"], d["who"], (": " + d["why"]) if d["why"] else "")
        return r

    m = ridx.get(norm(r["company"])) if r["company"] not in ("", "-") else None
    if m and m["sent"] >= r["seen"]:
        if m["confirmed"]:
            r["state"], r["why"] = "discharged", "reply-state vid %s sent %s, thread confirmed" % (
                m["vid"], iso(m["sent"]))
            return r
        r["state"], r["why"] = "sent-unconfirmed", (
            "reply-state vid %s sent %s but the thread never confirmed — a click is not an answer"
            % (m["vid"], iso(m["sent"])))
        return r

    r["state"] = "over" if age_h > r["bound_h"] else "open"
    r["why"] = "no discharge: %s" % ("no company on the row, so the reply tape cannot be joined"
                                     if r["company"] in ("", "-") else
                                     "company %r not answered in reply-state" % r["company"])
    return r


def coverage(rows, ridx):
    """How much of the non-code corpus the ONE machine discharge leg can even reach. Published
    every run because a join that silently covers a tenth of its subject is the failure this tool
    was built out of."""
    subj = [r for r in rows if r["kind"] != "code"]
    named = [r for r in subj if r["company"] not in ("", "-")]
    joined = [r for r in named if norm(r["company"]) in ridx]
    return {"non_code": len(subj), "named": len(named), "joinable": len(joined)}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- intake coverage

# THE COUNT AND ITS DENOMINATOR TRAVEL TOGETHER. This tool answers «is anything awaiting a hand»,
# and until 2026-08-29 it answered it over whatever `job-mail.log` happened to contain — with no
# way to tell an empty inbox from an inbox only half of which was read. mesh-job-mail grew a lane
# tape and a dark-episode alarm for exactly that, but a sidecar cannot narrow a bit its readers
# never open: measured that day, `job-mail-lanes.log` had ONE reader in the whole genome, its own
# writer, while this file and mesh-dash went on rendering «nothing awaiting a hand» with the gmail
# leg 87 up-hours dark. So the lane verdict is asked here, from the producer that owns the
# arithmetic — never re-derived, because two implementations of «how dark» drift apart and the
# quieter one wins the argument by being printed first.
LANES_TOOL = "mesh-job-mail"


def lane_degradation(runner=None, timeout=25):
    """-> (clauses, err). Each clause names ONE leg that is not delivering and for how long, so a
    caller can paste them beside its own count. The list being EMPTY is itself a claim — that
    every leg is standing and the reading is current — so every way of failing to establish that
    returns a clause instead: tool absent, non-zero, unparseable, timed out. Never [] on doubt."""
    run = runner or (lambda: subprocess.run([LANES_TOOL, "--lanes", "--json"],
                                            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout))
    try:
        p = run()
    except Exception as e:
        return ["lane state UNKNOWN (%s --lanes: %s)" % (LANES_TOOL, e)], str(e)
    if p.returncode != 0:
        return (["lane state UNKNOWN (%s --lanes rc=%s)" % (LANES_TOOL, p.returncode)],
                (p.stderr or "").strip()[:200])
    try:
        rd = json.loads(p.stdout)
    except Exception as e:
        return ["lane state UNKNOWN (%s --lanes unparseable: %s)" % (LANES_TOOL, e)], str(e)
    if not rd.get("tape_present"):
        return ["lane state UNKNOWN (no lane tape)"], None
    if rd.get("stale") is not False:
        why = ("evidence %.1f up-h old, past the %.1f up-h bound"
               % (rd.get("age_up_h") or 0.0, rd.get("stale_bound_uph") or 0.0)
               if rd.get("stale") else "freshness bound %s" % rd.get("stale_bound_src"))
        return ["lane state UNKNOWN (%s)" % why], None
    out = []
    for ln, fr in sorted((rd.get("lanes") or {}).items()):
        if not fr or fr.get("state") == "unknown":
            out.append("%s UNKNOWN" % ln)
        elif fr.get("state") == "dark":
            uph = fr.get("dark_uph")
            how = ("%s%.1f up-h" % (">=" if fr.get("uph_is_floor") else "", uph)
                   if uph is not None else "unknown up-h")
            ll = fr.get("last_live")
            out.append("%s DARK %s (%s run(s), last live %s)"
                       % (ln, how, fr.get("dark_runs"), iso(ll) if ll else "never"))
    return out, None


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- rendering

def age_str(r):
    bits = ["%.1fh cal" % r["age_h"]]
    if r["up_h"] is None:
        bits.append("up-h na (boot record unreadable)")
    else:
        bits.append("%s%.1f up-h" % (">=" if r["up_floor"] else "", r["up_h"]))
    return " / ".join(bits)


def line(r):
    who = r["company"] if r["company"] not in ("", "-") else (r["subject"] or "?")[:48]
    role = (" — " + r["role"][:48]) if r["role"] not in ("", "-") else ""
    return "  %-9s %-11s %s%s :: %s (bound %.1fh %s) :: %s" % (
        r["state"].upper(), r["uid"], who, role, age_str(r), r["bound_h"], r["bound_src"], r["why"])


def should_print(quiet, err, rows, degraded):
    """--quiet means «say nothing unless something needs a hand». A leg being down IS something
    that needs a hand, and the failure direction of getting this wrong is total silence: the pane
    and the cron log would both stay empty for the entire eight days the intake was half blind."""
    if not quiet:
        return True
    return bool(err) or any(r["state"] == "over" for r in rows) or bool(degraded)


def render(rows, cov, err, now, degraded=None):
    degraded = [] if degraded is None else degraded
    intake = ("\nINTAKE DEGRADED — the corpus below is NOT the whole inbox: "
              + "; ".join(degraded)) if degraded else ""
    if err is not None:
        return ("UNKNOWN — job-mail.log unreadable (%s). This is NOT «no incoming»." % err) + intake
    by = {}
    for r in rows:
        by[r["state"]] = by.get(r["state"], 0) + 1
    live = [r for r in rows if r["state"] in ("over", "open", "sent-unconfirmed")]
    live.sort(key=lambda r: r["seen"])
    head = "act rows: %d total · %s" % (
        len(rows), " · ".join("%s=%d" % (k, v) for k, v in sorted(by.items())) or "none")
    head += "\nmachine discharge leg (reply-state by employer name) reaches %d/%d non-code rows" \
            " (%d carry a company at all) — the rest can only be discharged explicitly" % (
                cov["joinable"], cov["non_code"], cov["named"])
    head += intake
    if not live:
        # THE FORBIDDEN SENTENCE. «Nothing awaiting a hand» is an assertion about the world, and
        # with a leg down it is an assertion the corpus cannot support — that is the exact shape
        # in which one unplugged phone made eight days of employer mail read as an empty inbox.
        # With a leg dark it degrades to a claim about the legs that were actually read.
        return head + ("\nnothing awaiting a hand IN THE LEGS THAT WERE READ — and the ones above"
                       " were not, so this is NOT «nothing came in»." if degraded
                       else "\nnothing awaiting a hand.")
    oldest = live[0]
    head += "\noldest awaiting a hand: %s, %s" % (oldest["uid"], age_str(oldest))
    return head + "\n" + "\n".join(line(r) for r in live) + \
        "\ndischarge with: mesh-job-act --discharge <uid> --verb %s [--why ...]" % "|".join(VERBS)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- tapes

def append(path, fields):
    try:
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        with open(path, "a") as f:
            f.write("\t".join(str(x) for x in fields) + "\n")
        return True
    except Exception as e:
        print("tape unwritable: %s: %s" % (path, e), file=sys.stderr)
        return False


def run_row(path, rows, cov, err, now, degraded=None):
    """UNCONDITIONAL, every pass, open or not — the silences in this tape are the denominator
    that separates «this reflex ran and found nothing» from «this reflex has not run».

    The intake field is APPENDED, never inserted: mesh-dash reads this tape by cut -f4/-f5/-f6 and
    a column added in the middle would silently re-point every one of them at its neighbour. It
    is the last field for the same reason it exists at all — the pane is the reader that has to
    stop rendering «входящих без ответа: 0» over a corpus that was only half read, and it reads
    the TAPE, not the tool (an up-hours leg shelling out to journalctl per frame would wedge it)."""
    intake = "intake=" + (";".join(degraded).replace("\t", " ") if degraded else "ok")
    if err is not None:
        return append(path, [iso(now), "UNKNOWN", "ledger-unreadable", err[:120], intake])
    c = {}
    for r in rows:
        c[r["state"]] = c.get(r["state"], 0) + 1
    over = [r for r in rows if r["state"] == "over"]
    oldest = max((r["age_h"] for r in over), default=0.0)
    return append(path, [iso(now), "RUN",
                         "total=%d" % len(rows),
                         ",".join("%s=%d" % kv for kv in sorted(c.items())) or "none",
                         "oldest_over_h=%.1f" % oldest,
                         "join_cov=%d/%d" % (cov["joinable"], cov["non_code"]),
                         intake])


def speak(rows, now, tg, state_path=ALARM, emit=None):
    """One board line per pass that has something over its bound, throttled per KEY so a row that
    stays open does not repost every hour — but it DOES repost after the throttle, because the
    whole defect was a one-shot notification that scrolled away. Returns the text or None."""
    over = sorted([r for r in rows if r["state"] == "over"], key=lambda r: r["seen"])
    if not over:
        return None
    try:
        st = json.loads(pathlib.Path(state_path).read_text())
    except Exception:
        st = {}
    due = [r for r in over
           if (now - (st.get(r["key"], {}).get("at") or 0)) / 3600.0 >= THROTTLE_H]
    if not due:
        return None
    held = len(over) - len(due)
    o = over[0]
    who = o["company"] if o["company"] not in ("", "-") else (o["subject"] or "?")[:40]
    txt = ("[fyi] job-act: %d act-строк(и) без ответа сверх своего порога, старейшая %s (%s, %s) — "
           "письмо разобрано и записано, но НИКТО НЕ ОТВЕТИЛ. %s%s\n%s\n"
           "разрядить: mesh-job-act --discharge <uid> --verb %s" % (
               len(over), o["uid"], who, age_str(o),
               "новых с прошлого поста: %d" % len(due),
               (", ещё %d придержаны троттлом %.0fh" % (held, THROTTLE_H)) if held else "",
               "\n".join(line(r) for r in due), "|".join(VERBS)))
    if emit is not None:
        emit(txt)
    else:
        # A missing mesh-chat raises FileNotFoundError, not a return code, and an unguarded raise
        # here would kill the pass AFTER the liability was found and BEFORE the throttle cursor
        # was written — the loudest moment turned into the one that reports nothing.
        for cmd in ([CHAT, txt],) + (([TG, txt.replace("[fyi] ", "")],) if tg else ()):
            try:
                subprocess.run(cmd, check=False, timeout=60)
            except Exception as e:
                print("could not speak via %s: %s" % (cmd[0], e), file=sys.stderr)
    for r in due:
        st[r["key"]] = {"at": now, "age_h": round(r["age_h"], 1)}
    try:
        pathlib.Path(state_path).parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        pathlib.Path(state_path).write_text(json.dumps(st, indent=1))
    except Exception as e:
        print("alarm state unwritable: %s" % e, file=sys.stderr)
    return txt


def do_discharge(uid, verb, why, who, now, rows_path=None, dis_path=None):
    """Resolve a bare uid to exactly one row and write an attributed discharge. An ambiguous or
    unknown uid is REFUSED, never guessed at: a discharge is the one write here that silences a
    liability, so it must never land on a row nobody named."""
    if verb not in VERBS:
        print("verb must be one of: %s" % ", ".join(VERBS), file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    rows, err = act_rows(rows_path)
    if err is not None:
        print("job-mail.log unreadable: %s" % err, file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    hits = [r for r in rows if r["key"] == uid or r["uid"] == uid]
    if not hits:
        print("no act row with uid/key %r (%d act rows known)" % (uid, len(rows)), file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if len(hits) > 1:
        print("ambiguous: %r matches %d rows — name the full key: %s"
              % (uid, len(hits), ", ".join(r["key"] for r in hits)), file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    r = hits[0]
    if not append(dis_path or DISCHARGE, [iso(now), r["key"], who, verb, why]):
        return 2
    print("discharged %s (%s) as %s by %s%s" % (r["key"], r["kind"], verb, who,
                                                (": " + why) if why else ""))
    return 0


